<VI _name="lvai_wire_dyn_events.vi" description="Wire a VI's event registration refnum onto its Event Structure's DYNAMIC EVENT terminal - the one connection AIXML cannot express, and therefore the only thing a regeneration loses.\0A\0ATWO SOURCES, TRIED IN THAT ORDER. Preferred\3A the structure's own Event Registration Refnum TUNNEL, whose net is BRANCHED - authoring that tunnel in AIXML is what makes the whole route survive a regeneration, so it is the shape to write. Fallback\3A the Register For Events node on the block diagram, wired straight across the loop border, which LabVIEW accepts and answers by creating the tunnel itself - measured. The fallback assumes ONE such node in the VI and reports how many it saw.\0A\0AHOW BOTH ENDS ARE IDENTIFIED - BY NAME, never by index. The structure's terminals answer to the literal Event Registration Refnum, the node's to the lower-case event registration refnum, because a terminal's name comes from its data type or its node's label rather than from a user label. The node's refnum output is picked as 'a source that is not named error out', which needs no spelling of the refnum at all.\0A\0AAuto Route? (F) IS WIRED TRUE, AND THAT IS NOT OPTIONAL. With its FALSE default the connection is registered in every readable form - signal termList, the terminal's bit 15, a grown compressedWireTable, a live Connected Wire, Is Broken? false, the VI unbroken - and LabVIEW's renderer draws NOTHING. Position and Bounds are identical between the two cases, so no property can tell them apart and CleanUpWire changes neither.\0A\0AWHAT IT REPORTS AS THE EFFECT is the DESTINATION terminal's own wire\3A none before, one that is not broken after. That is route-independent, where an end COUNT is not - branching the tunnel's net gives a wire of three ends and crossing the loop border gives a fresh one of two.\0A\0ATHE SEARCH IS AT ANY DEPTH\3A NI's Traverse for GObjects.vi walks the whole block diagram with Class Name EventStructure, and again with RegisterForEvents for the fallback source - the same call lvai_swap_subvis.xml makes for SubVI, where it was measured reaching calls inside Case frames inside a loop. It replaces a hand-written search that went two levels deep and therefore missed an Event Structure in a Case frame in a While Loop, the standard Actor Framework helper-loop shape. Class Name carries NO SPACES (EventStructure). Traverse returns GObject references, so the structure is cast with To More Specific Class. With more than one Event Structure the FIRST reference is wired and the count is reported\3B which one Traverse lists first is not measured. The class names on the top-level diagram are still reported, as the evidence behind a miss.\0A\0ATHE RIGHT SIDE, unless wire right side is FALSE\3A the structure's SOURCE terminal named Event Registration Refnum is wired to the event registration refnum input of Unregister For Events, Auto Wire and Auto Route TRUE, and LabVIEW makes the tunnels on the way out - the shape a run on an Actor Framework helper loop measured. The Unregister node is found by Traverse with Class Name Function, kept only when its Class Name is exactly Function and it has a SINK terminal of that lower-case name, because Unregister For Events has no class of its own. None, more than one, an input already wired, or no source terminal - the Case Structure skips Connect Wire and the answer says which. It runs on its OWN error branch\3A the left side's error passes through the case untouched and feeds the save, so a right-side failure is reported and does not throw away the left wire. A tunnel this creates on an enclosing Case Structure is UNWIRED in every other frame and breaks the VI\3B Use Default if Unwired is refused on an AIXML Property Node, so the tool sets it afterwards in the pylabview bundle, not here.\0A\0AONE PROPERTY NODE PER REFERENCE, and no Connected Wire on a terminal not established as wired\3A that read is what invalidated the references following it in a probe, and it is why the tunnel branch here runs on an error path of its own.">
